.TH VI 1 "September 30, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
vi \- screen oriented (visual) display editor based on ex
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vi
[
.I file
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Vi
(visual) is a display oriented text editor based on
.IR ex (1).
.I Ex
and
.I vi
run the same code; it is possible to get to
the command mode of
.I ex
from within
.I vi
and vice-versa.
.PP
The
.I "Vi Quick Reference"
card and the
.I "Introduction to Display Editing with Vi"
describe the full Berkeley editor.
What follows is the subset this
.I vi
implements.
Changes affect the buffer, not the file, until it is written.
.PP
.B Modes.
Start in command mode.
i inserts text; Escape returns to command mode.
:wq followed by Return writes and leaves; :q! discards the changes.
ZZ writes if modified and quits.
.PP
.B Motion.
h j k l move left, down, up and right.
w b e move by words; W B E use whitespace-delimited words.
0 goes to column zero, ^ to the first nonblank, $ to the last
character.
G goes to the last line; 1G to the first; a count before G selects
that line.
f/F find the next/previous character on the line; t/T stop short of
it; ; repeats the find and , reverses it.
Most motions accept a leading decimal count.
.PP
.B Changes.
i/a insert before/after the cursor; I/A at the first nonblank/end.
o/O open a line below/above.
d, c and y followed by a motion delete, change or yank its span;
the motions include /pattern, ?pattern, n and N, which end the span at
the match (the match itself is not included);
dd, cc and yy operate on lines.
x/X delete at/before the cursor; D/C act through end of line;
Y yanks a line; s/S substitute characters/lines.
p/P put the unnamed register after/before the cursor.
A span that crosses lines is kept whole, characterwise, and is put back
with its line breaks.
.PP
.B Registers.
"a through "z before a yank, delete, change or put name a register:
"ayy and "add store into register a, "ap and "aP put from it.
The unnamed register then holds whatever was last used.
Capital names ("A to "Z, append) and the numbered registers ("1 to
"9) are not implemented, and neither is any other name; they, a
register name followed by a command that is not a yank, delete, change
or put, and a put from an empty register all ring the bell and change
nothing.
All the registers share one 3699-byte area; a yank that does not fit
rings the bell and leaves that register empty, and so does a span of
more than 250 lines.
J joins lines, r replaces one character, and ~ changes case.
u undoes the last change; another u redoes it.
.PP
.B Searching and ex commands.
/pattern and ?pattern followed by Return search forward and backward;
n repeats the search and N reverses it.
The basic regular expressions are those of ed(1).
A colon command is executed by the
.I ex
engine: :w, :wq, :q!, :e file, :1,$s/old/new/g and the rest.
.PP
Control-Z suspends the editor and restores the shell's terminal
state; fg resumes it.
The editor uses an alternate screen and restores the terminal on a
normal exit; reset(1) recovers from an abnormal one.
.PP
Text is held in an EX.SWPxx swap file in the working directory, so
a file may be larger than process memory, within the line-table
limit below.
.SH FILES
See
.IR ex (1).
.br
/usr/man/VI  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
